{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.QCharacter","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.QCharacter","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.QCharacter.IsQChar.ratio_qInvariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.QCharacter.IsQChar.ratio_qInvariant","kind":"theorem","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QCharacter.lean#L66-L75","doc":"**The ellipticity source (why the Birkhoff matrix is theta-valued).** If `f` and `g` are\ntwo q-characters with the *same* nonzero multiplier `c`, their ratio `f/g` is `q`-invariant\n(elliptic). The connection matrix `P = (X^∞)⁻¹X^0` compares two solutions with matching\nexponents, so its entries are such ratios — elliptic, hence theta quotients, not constants. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.QCharacter.c1_det_character","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.QCharacter.c1_det_character","kind":"theorem","line":57,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QCharacter.lean#L57-L64","doc":"**C1 determinant character.** The product of the two C1 exponent characters (multipliers\n`λ₊, λ₋` with `λ₊·λ₋ = q−1 = det A(0)`, per `C1ModuleExponents`) is the determinant character\n`e_{q,q−1}` — the q-character carried by `det X^{(0)}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.QCharacter.isQChar_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.QCharacter.isQChar_one","kind":"theorem","line":53,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QCharacter.lean#L53-L55","doc":"The constant `1` is the trivial q-character `e_{q,1}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.QCharacter.IsQChar.mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.QCharacter.IsQChar.mul","kind":"theorem","line":46,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QCharacter.lean#L46-L51","doc":"**q-characters multiply**: `e_{q,c}·e_{q,d}` is a q-character with multiplier `c·d`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.QCharacter.QInvariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.QCharacter.QInvariant","kind":"def","line":41,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QCharacter.lean#L41-L42","doc":"**q-invariant (elliptic)**: `h(q·x) = h(x)`, i.e. a function on `ℂ*/qℤ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.QCharacter.IsQChar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.QCharacter.IsQChar","kind":"def","line":38,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/QCharacter.lean#L38-L39","doc":"**q-character**: `f(q·x) = c·f(x)`. The multiplier `c` is the local exponent. ","signatureChunk":0}]}